from retained set). Then continue with the next node in the sorted list. Note: after removing a node, its parent may become eligible (if all its children are now removed). But since we process deeper first, when we get to the parent later, its children are already removed, so condition passes. However note: the removal of a node also removes its entire subtree? Actually if a node is removed, all its descendants are already removed (since we are doing bottom-up, children must be removed first). So the subtree is gone. So it's fine.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Parse nodes list into dict by id.\n- Validate structure: root has parent null, depth consistent? Not strictly required but can check. For simplicity, assume valid.\n- Build children list for each node.\n- Determine initial retained set = all node IDs.\n- Determine required symbol set from required_symbols list.\n- Create list of candidate nodes (all nodes except root, and those with removable=True? Actually we consider all nodes, but only attempt removal if removable=True. The condition \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we will filter on the fly.\n- Sort candidates by depth descending, then by id ascending (string comparison).\n- removal_order = [].\n- For each node in sorted candidates:\n    if node.removable is False: continue\n    if node.id == root id: continue\n    if node.symbol in required_symbols: continue\n    # Check if any of its children are still retained (i.e., in retained set)\n    children = children_by_parent[node.id]\n    if any(child_id in retained for child_id in children):\n        continue\n    # Tentatively remove: check required symbols\n    # Simulate removal: remove node from retained set temporarily? Actually we need to check if after removal, all required symbols still exist in retained set.\n    # Since we are removing only this node, and its children are already gone, the only symbol that might be lost is this node's symbol.\n    required_symbols_set = set(required_symbols)\n    # Compute current symbols in retained set (including this node). We can count symbols.\n    # Since we only remove this node, we just need to ensure that node.symbol is either not required, or if required, there is another node with that symbol in retained set.\n    # But careful: could be multiple nodes with same symbol. So we need to check if after removal, every required symbol still has at least one node.\n    # We can maintain a counter of symbols. Initially count symbol frequencies in retained set. Then for each removal, decrement count for that symbol. If after decrement, for any required symbol count becomes 0, then removal invalid.\n    # So we need to maintain symbol_counts.\n    # Since nodes are processed in order, we can update counts dynamically.\n    symbol_counts = defaultdict(int)\n    for node_id in retained:\n        symbol_counts[nodes_by_id[node_id].symbol] += 1\n    # Then for candidate:\n    symbol = node.symbol\n    if symbol in required_symbols_set and symbol_counts[symbol] == 1:\n        continue # removal would remove last occurrence\n    # Else remove:\n    removal_order.append(node.id)\n    retained.remove(node.id)\n    symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1\n    # Note: children are already removed, so their symbols already subtracted.\n- After loop, sort retained IDs (list(retained) sorted).\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": sorted_retained, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nEdge cases: root node has removable=False, so not removed. Required symbols: root is always required? Actually required_symbols includes \"root\". So root is required, so cannot be removed anyway.\n\nCheck feasibility: if any required symbol not present initially, raise ValueError? But it's given valid.\n\nAlso need to handle case where children list might include nodes that are already removed? But we build children list once. When we check children retained, we look at current retained set. That works.\n\nPotential issue: What if a node has children that are not removable? They will never be removed, so parent cannot be removed. That's fine.\n\nAlso note: The instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That's exactly our check.\n\nReturn value: JSON-serializable object: dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". The function solve(case) returns that.\n\nNow encode as JSON string. Must escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. Use triple-quoted string? Better to write the Python code as a string with escaped newlines, or embed it in JSON directly. Since we are to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being a JSON string containing the Python source. So double-quote the Python source, escape all double quotes and backslashes and newlines.\n\nLet me write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    nodes_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children list\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n        else:\n            children[parent].append(n['id'])\n    # Initial retained set\n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\n    # Compute symbol counts\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    symbol_counts = defaultdict(int)\n    for n in nodes:\n        symbol_counts[n['symbol']] += 1\n    # Prepare candidates sorted by depth descending, then id ascending\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removal_order = []\n    for n in candidates:\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['id'] == root_id:\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[n['symbol']] == 1:\n            # Removing would delete the only occurrence of a required symbol\n            continue\n        # Check all children are already removed (not in retained)\n        child_ids = children.get(n['id'], [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removal_order.append(n['id'])\n        retained.remove(n['id'])\n        symbol_counts[n['symbol']] -= 1\n    # Sort retained IDs\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {'retained_ids': retained_ids, 'removal_order': removal_order}\n```\n\nBut note: The symbol count check using only symbol_counts[n['symbol']] == 1 is insufficient if there are multiple required symbols and the node's symbol is not required but its removal might affect something? No, only required symbols matter. However there could be a situation where a required symbol appears only in a node that is not removable? That's fine. Our check only prevents removal if the node's symbol is required and it's the only occurrence. But what if the node's symbol is not required, but another required symbol is only represented by a node that is a child of this node? But we already ensure children are removed first. So if the child is removed, it must have passed its own check. If a required symbol is only present in a child that is removable, that child will be blocked because its symbol count would drop to zero. So the child will not be removed. Then the parent cannot be removed because child is still retained. So it's safe. However we also need to ensure that after removal of a node, any required symbol that is only present in its subtree (which is already removed) is already accounted. Our check on children retained ensures that if a required symbol exists in a child, that child is still retained, so parent cannot be removed. If the child is not required, it can be removed, and then parent can be removed if no other children retained. So the symbol count for the parent's symbol is the only thing that matters. So the check is correct.\n\nPotential nuance: The root's symbol is \"root\" and it's in required_symbols. Root is not removable, so fine.\n\nNow ensure we handle case where there is no root? But fixture has root.\n\nAlso need to import defaultdict inside function? It's standard library, allowed. But we can also use plain dict with get.\n\nBetter to use local variable to avoid import statement? Possibly use `from collections import defaultdict` at top. Or use a simple dict and increment. Simpler: just compute symbol_counts as a dict of symbol to count with integer, and use `if n['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[n['symbol']] == 1`. We can initialize symbol_counts with counts.\n\nWe also need to consider that nodes may have same id? No.\n\nWrite the code with proper indentation. Then escape.\n\nMake sure to use double quotes inside the code, so escape them with backslash.\n\nNow produce JSON string. The entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". I'll construct that.\n\nImplementation string: start with `{` then `\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    nodes_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n        else:\\n            children[parent].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    candidates = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for n in candidates:\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['id'] == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols and symbol_counts[n['symbol']] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(n['id'], [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_